%% Part III: Formal Semantics

%% ============================================================
%% 25.1 Introduction
%% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:denotational-intro}

Denotational semantics assigns to each program a mathematical object---its
\emph{denotation}---that represents its meaning.  Unlike operational
semantics, which describes \emph{how} a program executes, denotational
semantics describes \emph{what} a program means.  The meaning of a program
is a mathematical function, and the semantics is defined by composing these
functions.

Denotational semantics was introduced by Christopher Strachey in the 1960s,
building on the domain theory of Dana Scott.  It provides the most
mathematically rigorous approach to programming language semantics, and it
has deep connections to topology, order theory, and category theory.

In this chapter, we develop denotational semantics from the ground up.
We begin with mathematical domains and continuous functions.  We then
define semantic functions as mathematical objects and show how recursive
definitions are handled through fixed points.  We develop domains for
imperative and functional languages, discuss powerdomains for
nondeterminism, and examine the concept of full abstraction.

\begin{definition}[Denotational Semantics]\label{def:denotational}
  \emph{Denotational semantics} assigns to each syntactic construct a
  mathematical object (its \emph{denotation}) in such a way that the
  denotation of a compound construct is a function of the denotations of
  its parts.  This is the \emph{compositionality principle}:
  \[
    \sembrack{C[e_1, \ldots, e_n]} = F_C(\sembrack{e_1}, \ldots, \sembrack{e_n})
  \]
  where $C$ is a syntactic constructor and $F_C$ is a mathematical
  function.
\end{definition}

\begin{historical}
Denotational semantics originated in the work of Christopher Strachey at
the Oxford University Computing Laboratory in the 1960s.  Strachey's
``mathematical semantics'' aimed to give programming languages the same
rigorous foundation as mathematics.  Dana Scott's domain theory provided
the mathematical tools needed to handle recursive definitions and
nontermination.  The landmark work of Scott and Strachey was published in
the ``CSI Report'' of 1971.
\end{historical}

%% ============================================================
%% 25.2 Mathematical Domains and Continuous Functions
%% ============================================================
\section{Mathematical Domains and Continuous Functions}\label{sec:domains}

\subsection{Why Domains?}\label{sec:why-domains}

The standard mathematical functions (on sets) are insufficient for
programming language semantics because:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Nontermination}: Programs may not terminate.  We need a
    domain that includes a ``bottom'' element $\bottom$ representing
    nontermination.
  \item \textbf{Recursive definitions}: Functions can be defined in terms
    of themselves.  We need a mathematical framework for solving recursive
    equations.
  \item \textbf{Infinite data structures}: Lazy languages can produce
    infinite lists and trees.  We need domains that contain infinite
    elements.
  \item \textbf{Partial information}: During computation, we may have
    partial information about a value.  We need an ordering that captures
    ``less defined'' vs.\ ``more defined.''
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Partial Orders and Directed Sets}\label{sec:partial-orders}

\begin{definition}[Partial Order]\label{def:partial-order}
  A \emph{partial order} (poset) is a pair $(D, \sqsubseteq)$ where $D$ is
  a set and $\sqsubseteq$ is a binary relation on $D$ that is:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Reflexive}: $x \sqsubseteq x$ for all $x \in D$.
    \item \textbf{Antisymmetric}: If $x \sqsubseteq y$ and $y \sqsubseteq x$
      then $x = y$.
    \item \textbf{Transitive}: If $x \sqsubseteq y$ and $y \sqsubseteq z$
      then $x \sqsubseteq z$.
  \end{itemize}
\end{definition}

The ordering $\sqsubseteq$ is read ``is less defined than'' or ``approximates.''

\begin{definition}[Directed Set]\label{def:directed-set}
  A subset $S \subseteq D$ is \emph{directed} if it is non-empty and every
  finite subset of $S$ has an upper bound in $S$:
  \[
    \forall x, y \in S.\, \exists z \in S.\, x \sqsubseteq z \wedge y \sqsubseteq z
  \]
\end{definition}

\subsection{Complete Partial Orders (CPOs)}\label{sec:cpos}

\begin{definition}[CPO]\label{def:cpo}
  A \emph{complete partial order} (CPO, or $\omega$-CPO) is a partial
  order $(D, \sqsubseteq)$ with a least element $\bottom$ such that every
  directed subset $S \subseteq D$ has a least upper bound $\bigsqcup S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Pointed CPO (CPO$_\bot$)]\label{def:pointed-cpo}
  A \emph{pointed CPO} is a CPO that has a least element $\bottom$.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Common Domains]\label{ex:domains}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item $\Nat_\bot = \{0, 1, 2, \ldots\} \cup \{\bottom\}$: Natural
      numbers with nontermination.  $\bottom \sqsubseteq n$ for all $n$.
    \item $\Bool_\bot = \{\mathbf{true}, \mathbf{false}, \bottom\}$:
      Booleans with nontermination.
    \item $\Sigma^*$: Finite and infinite strings, ordered by prefix.
    \item $\Nat_\bot^\omega$: Infinite sequences of lifted naturals.
  \end{itemize}
\end{example}

\subsection{Continuous Functions}\label{sec:continuous-functions}

\begin{definition}[Monotone Function]\label{def:monotone}
  A function $f : D \to E$ between posets is \emph{monotone} (or
  \emph{order-preserving}) if:
  \[
    x \sqsubseteq y \implies f(x) \sqsubseteq f(y)
  \]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Continuous Function]\label{def:continuous}
  A function $f : D \to E$ between CPOs is \emph{continuous} (or
  \emph{Scott-continuous}) if it preserves directed suprema:
  \[
    f\left(\bigsqcup S\right) = \bigsqcup f(S) \quad \text{for all directed } S \subseteq D
  \]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Continuous = Monotone + Chain-Continuous]\label{thm:continuous-equiv}
  A function between CPOs is continuous if and only if it is monotone and
  preserves suprema of $\omega$-chains:
  \[
    f\left(\bigsqcup_{n \in \omega} x_n\right) = \bigsqcup_{n \in \omega} f(x_n)
  \]
  for every ascending chain $x_0 \sqsubseteq x_1 \sqsubseteq x_2 \sqsubseteq \ldots$
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Function Spaces}\label{sec:function-spaces}

\begin{definition}[Function Space]\label{def:function-space}
  The \emph{function space} $[D \to E]$ is the set of all continuous
  functions from $D$ to $E$, ordered pointwise:
  \[
    f \sqsubseteq g \iff \forall x \in D.\, f(x) \sqsubseteq g(x)
  \]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Function Space Theorem]\label{thm:function-space}
  If $D$ and $E$ are CPOs, then $[D \to E]$ is a CPO.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Product and Sum Domains}\label{sec:product-sum}

\begin{definition}[Product Domain]\label{def:product-domain}
  The \emph{product} of CPOs $D_1$ and $D_2$ is:
  \[
    D_1 \times D_2 = \{ (d_1, d_2) \mid d_1 \in D_1, d_2 \in D_2 \}
  \]
  with componentwise ordering: $(d_1, d_2) \sqsubseteq (d_1', d_2')$ iff
  $d_1 \sqsubseteq d_1'$ and $d_2 \sqsubseteq d_2'$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Lifted Domain]\label{def:lifted-domain}
  The \emph{lift} of a set $S$ is:
  \[
    S_\bot = S \cup \{\bot\}
  \]
  with $\bot \sqsubseteq s$ for all $s \in S$ and $s \sqsubseteq s$ for all
  $s \in S$.
\end{definition}

%% ============================================================
%% 25.3 Semantic Functions as Mathematical Objects
%% ============================================================
\section{Semantic Functions as Mathematical Objects}\label{sec:semantic-functions}

\subsection{The Semantic Function}\label{sec:semantic-function}

In denotational semantics, each syntactic category is assigned a
\emph{semantic function} that maps programs to their denotations:

\begin{definition}[Semantic Function]\label{def:semantic-function}
  A \emph{semantic function} is a continuous function from syntax to
  meaning:
  \begin{align*}
    \semE & : \Expr \to \Env \to \Val \\
    \semS & : \Stmt \to \State \to \State \\
    \semD & : \Decl \to \Env \to \Env
  \end{align*}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Semantic Functions for Arithmetic}\label{sec:semantic-arith}

\begin{grammar}{Arithmetic Expressions}
  \production{a}{n \alt x \alt a_1 + a_2}
\end{grammar}

\begin{align*}
  \semE \sembrack{n} &= \lambda \rho.\, n \\
  \semE \sembrack{x} &= \lambda \rho.\, \rho(x) \\
  \semE \sembrack{a_1 + a_2} &= \lambda \rho.\, \semE \sembrack{a_1} \rho + \semE \sembrack{a_2} \rho
\end{align*}

\subsection{Semantic Functions for Commands}\label{sec:semantic-commands}

\begin{grammar}{Commands}
  \production{S}{\mathbf{skip} \alt x \coloneqq a \alt S_1 ; S_2 \alt \mathbf{if}\ b\ \mathbf{then}\ S_1\ \mathbf{else}\ S_2 \alt \mathbf{while}\ b\ \mathbf{do}\ S}
\end{grammar}

\begin{align*}
  \semS \sembrack{\mathbf{skip}} &= \lambda \sigma.\, \sigma \\
  \semS \sembrack{x \coloneqq a} &= \lambda \sigma.\, \sigma[x \mapsto \semE \sembrack{a} \sigma] \\
  \semS \sembrack{S_1 ; S_2} &= \lambda \sigma.\, \semS \sembrack{S_2} (\semS \sembrack{S_1} \sigma) \\
  \semS \sembrack{\mathbf{if}\ b\ \mathbf{then}\ S_1\ \mathbf{else}\ S_2} &= \lambda \sigma.\, \begin{cases}
    \semS \sembrack{S_1} \sigma & \text{if } \semB \sembrack{b} \sigma = \mathbf{true} \\
    \semS \sembrack{S_2} \sigma & \text{if } \semB \sembrack{b} \sigma = \mathbf{false}
  \end{cases}
\end{align*}

\subsection{The Semantic Function for While}\label{sec:semantic-while}

The while loop is the most challenging construct because it involves
recursion:

\[
  \semS \sembrack{\mathbf{while}\ b\ \mathbf{do}\ S} = \fixpoint{W}
\]

where $W : (\State \to \State) \to (\State \to \State)$ is defined by:

\[
  W = \lambda f.\, \lambda \sigma.\, \begin{cases}
    \sigma & \text{if } \semB \sembrack{b} \sigma = \mathbf{false} \\
    f(\semS \sembrack{S} \sigma) & \text{if } \semB \sembrack{b} \sigma = \mathbf{true}
  \end{cases}
\]

The semantics of the while loop is the \emph{least fixed point} of $W$.

%% ============================================================
%% 25.4 Recursive Definitions and Fixed Points
%% ============================================================
\section{Recursive Definitions and Fixed Points}\label{sec:fixed-points}

\subsection{The Fixed-Point Theorem}\label{sec:fixed-point-theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Knaster--Tarski / Fixed-Point Theorem]\label{thm:fixed-point}
  Every continuous function $f : D \to D$ on a pointed CPO $D$ has a
  \emph{least fixed point}:
  \[
    \lfp(f) = \bigsqcup_{n \in \omega} f^n(\bottom)
  \]
  where $f^0(\bottom) = \bottom$ and $f^{n+1}(\bottom) = f(f^n(\bottom))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
  The sequence $\bottom \sqsubseteq f(\bottom) \sqsubseteq f^2(\bottom)
  \sqsubseteq \ldots$ is an ascending chain because $f$ is monotone and
  $\bottom$ is the least element.  Since $D$ is a CPO, the chain has a
  supremum $d^* = \bigsqcup_n f^n(\bottom)$.  By continuity of $f$:
  \[
    f(d^*) = f\left(\bigsqcup_n f^n(\bottom)\right) = \bigsqcup_n f^{n+1}(\bottom) = d^*
  \]
  So $d^*$ is a fixed point.  It is the least because any fixed point $d$
  satisfies $f^n(\bottom) \sqsubseteq d$ for all $n$ (by induction).
\end{proof}

\subsection{The Fixed-Point Combinator}\label{sec:fix-combinator}

\begin{definition}[Fixed-Point Combinator]\label{def:fix}
  The \emph{fixed-point combinator} $\fixpoint{}$ is the functional:
  \[
    \fixpoint{} : [D \to D] \to D
  \]
  defined by $\fixpoint{(f)} = \lfp(f)$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Solving Recursive Semantic Equations}\label{sec:solving-recursive}

The semantics of recursive constructs is defined as the least fixed point
of a functional:

\begin{example}[Factorial]\label{ex:factorial-denot}
  The factorial function is the least fixed point of:
  \[
    F = \lambda f.\, \lambda n.\, \begin{cases}
      1 & \text{if } n = 0 \\
      n \times f(n-1) & \text{if } n > 0 \\
      \bottom & \text{if } n = \bottom
    \end{cases}
  \]
  The iterations are:
  \begin{align*}
    F^0(\bottom) &= \lambda n.\, \bottom \\
    F^1(\bottom) &= \lambda n.\, \begin{cases} 1 & n = 0 \\ \bottom & \text{otherwise} \end{cases} \\
    F^2(\bottom) &= \lambda n.\, \begin{cases} 1 & n = 0 \\ 2 & n = 1 \\ \bottom & \text{otherwise} \end{cases} \\
    &\vdots \\
    \lfp(F) &= \lambda n.\, n!
  \end{align*}
\end{example}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Tabulating a Loop Denotation by Backward Propagation}

The least fixed point of a loop functional can sometimes be
computed without rescanning the whole function table on each
iteration. Fix a finite set $D$ of stores, a total guard $b$, and
a total deterministic body map $u:D\to D$. The table represents a
function from $D$ to the flat lifting $D_\bot$: a bottom entry
contains no result, while a defined entry contains the final store.
An edge from $s$ to $u(s)$ matters only when $b(s)$ holds.
Build reverse lists of these edges so a newly discovered result
can be propagated directly to stores that depend on it.

\begin{sudocode}[Compute all defined entries of a loop denotation]
\Function{DenoteLoop}{$D,b,u$}
  \State Set all $T$ entries to $\bot$; start empty lists and queue
  \ForAll{$s\in D$}
    \If{$b(s)$}
      \State Append $s$ to $\mathrm{pred}[u(s)]$
    \Else
      \State $T[s]\gets s$; enqueue $s$ \Comment{Exit fact}
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \While{the queue is not empty}
    \State $v\gets$ dequeue next store
    \ForAll{$s\in\mathrm{pred}[v]$ with $T[s]=\bot$}
      \State $T[s]\gets T[v]$; enqueue $s$ \Comment{Inherit result}
    \EndFor
  \EndWhile
  \State \Return $T$
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

\paragraph{Worked trace.}
Use stores $0,1,2,3$ with guard $s\ne0$, and true-guard updates
$u(1)=0$, $u(2)=1$, $u(3)=3$; let $u(0)=0$.
Initialization defines only $T[0]=0$. Dequeuing $0$ visits
predecessor $1$ and sets $T[1]=0$. Dequeuing $1$ sets
$T[2]=0$; dequeuing $2$ adds nothing. The self-loop at $3$
has no path to an exit and never enters the queue. The denotation
is $[0,0,0,\bot]$ in store order. Assigning $3$ to itself in the
result table would also satisfy that one recursive equation but
would not be the least solution.

\paragraph{Invariant and scope.}
A defined entry has a finite path to the exit it names, and all
such paths are covered by induction on their lengths. At
exhaustion the table is a fixed point, and every propagated
result is required in any fixed point, proving leastness.
There is at most one queue entry and one dependency edge per
store. This exact finite construction cannot classify bottom
entries by queue exhaustion on an arbitrary infinite store space.

\subsection{Existence and Uniqueness}\label{sec:existence-uniqueness}

\begin{theorem}[Existence of Least Fixed Point]\label{thm:lfp-existence}
  For every continuous function $f : D \to D$ on a pointed CPO, the least
  fixed point exists and is unique.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Well-Definedness of Semantics]\label{cor:well-defined}
  The denotational semantics of every programming language construct is
  well-defined, because every semantic equation has a unique least fixed
  point.
\end{corollary}

%% ============================================================
%% 25.5 Domains for Imperative and Functional Languages
%% ============================================================
\section{Domains for Imperative and Functional Languages}\label{sec:language-domains}

\subsection{Domains for Imperative Languages}\label{sec:imperative-domains}

The semantic domains for an imperative language are:

\begin{align*}
  \Val &= \Nat_\bot + \Bool_\bot + \ldots \quad \text{(values)} \\
  \Loc &= \{l_0, l_1, l_2, \ldots\} \quad \text{(locations)} \\
  \Store &= \Loc \to \Val_\bot \quad \text{(stores)} \\
  \State &= \Store \quad \text{(states = stores)} \\
  \Expr &\to \State \to \Val \quad \text{(expression semantics)} \\
  \Stmt &\to \State \to \State_\bot \quad \text{(statement semantics)}
\end{align*}

Note that statement semantics maps $\State \to \State_\bot$: the result may
be $\bottom$ (nontermination).

\subsection{Domains for Functional Languages}\label{sec:functional-domains}

For a functional language, the domains are:

\begin{align*}
  \Val &= \Nat_\bot + \Bool_\bot + [\Val \to \Val_\bot] + \ldots \\
  \Env &= \Var \to \Val_\bot \\
  \Expr &\to \Env \to \Val_\bot
\end{align*}

The key feature is the function space $[\Val \to \Val_\bot]$: values
include continuous functions from values to values.

\begin{example}[Domain for Lists]\label{ex:list-domain}
  The domain of lists of natural numbers is the solution of the domain
  equation:
  \[
    L \cong \{\bottom\} + \{()\} + (\Nat_\bot \times L)
  \]
  This domain contains:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item $\bottom$: nontermination (no information about the list).
    \item $()$: the empty list.
    \item $n : l$: a cons cell with head $n$ and tail $l$.
    \item Infinite lists: $1 : 2 : 3 : \ldots$
    \item Partial lists: $1 : \bottom$ (a list whose first element is 1
      but whose tail is undefined).
  \end{itemize}
\end{example}

\subsection{Recursive Domain Equations}\label{sec:recursive-domains}

\begin{definition}[Recursive Domain Equation]\label{def:recursive-domain}
  A \emph{recursive domain equation} is an equation of the form:
  \[
    D \cong F(D)
  \]
  where $F$ is a functor on the category of CPOs.  The solution is a
  fixed point of $F$.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Solving a Domain Equation]\label{ex:domain-equation}
  The domain of trees:
  \[
    T \cong \Nat_\bot + (T \times T)_\bot
  \]
  The solution is constructed as the colimit of the chain:
  \[
    \bottom \sqsubseteq F(\bottom) \sqsubseteq F^2(\bottom) \sqsubseteq \ldots
  \]
\end{example}

\subsection{Lazy vs. Strict Domains}\label{sec:lazy-strict}

\begin{definition}[Strict Function]\label{def:strict-function}
  A function $f : D \to E$ is \emph{strict} if $f(\bottom) = \bottom$.
\end{definition}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Lazy languages} (Haskell): Semantic functions are generally
    non-strict.  $f(\bottom) \neq \bottom$ is possible.
  \item \textbf{Strict languages} (ML, Scheme): Semantic functions are
    generally strict.  $f(\bottom) = \bottom$.
\end{itemize}

%% ============================================================
%% 25.6 Powerdomains for Nondeterminism
%% ============================================================
\section{Powerdomains for Nondeterminism}\label{sec:powerdomains}

\subsection{The Problem of Nondeterminism}\label{sec:nondeterminism-problem}

When a program can produce multiple results (due to nondeterminism,
concurrency, or abstract interpretation), we need a domain that represents
\emph{sets} of possible results.  The \emph{powerdomain} construction
provides this.

\subsection{Types of Powerdomains}\label{sec:powerdomain-types}

There are three main powerdomain constructions:

\begin{definition}[Plotkin Powerdomain]\label{def:plotkin-powerdomain}
  The \emph{Plotkin} (or \emph{convex}) \emph{powerdomain} $\mathcal{P}_P(D)$
  consists of the non-empty convex, closed subsets of $D$, ordered by
  \emph{Eggleston ordering}:
  \[
    A \sqsubseteq_P B \iff \forall a \in A.\, \exists b \in B.\, a \sqsubseteq b
    \;\wedge\; \forall b \in B.\, \exists a \in A.\, a \sqsubseteq b
  \]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Hoare Powerdomain]\label{def:hoare-powerdomain}
  The \emph{Hoare} (or \emph{upper}) \emph{powerdomain} $\mathcal{P}_H(D)$
  consists of the non-empty closed subsets of $D$, ordered by:
  \[
    A \sqsubseteq_H B \iff \forall a \in A.\, \exists b \in B.\, a \sqsubseteq b
  \]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Smyth Powerdomain]\label{def:smyth-powerdomain}
  The \emph{Smyth} (or \emph{lower}) \emph{powerdomain} $\mathcal{P}_S(D)$
  consists of the non-empty closed subsets of $D$, ordered by:
  \[
    A \sqsubseteq_S B \iff \forall b \in B.\, \exists a \in A.\, a \sqsubseteq b
  \]
\end{definition}

\subsection{When to Use Each Powerdomain}\label{sec:powerdomain-usage}

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Powerdomain Usage}
  \label{tab:powerdomains}
  \begin{tabular}{ll}
    \hline
    \textbf{Powerdomain} & \textbf{Usage} \\
    \hline
    Plotkin & Bounded nondeterminism (angelic + demonic) \\
    Hoare & Partial correctness (may-termination) \\
    Smyth & Total correctness (must-termination) \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Example: Nondeterministic Choice}\label{ex:nondet-choice}

Consider the program $x \coloneqq 1 \sqcap x \coloneqq 2$ (nondeterministic
choice):
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Plotkin}: The result is $\{1, 2\}$---both outcomes are
    possible.
  \item \textbf{Hoare}: The result is $\{1, 2\}$---we may get either.
  \item \textbf{Smyth}: The result is $\{1, 2\}$---we must get one of them.
\end{itemize}

%% ============================================================
%% 25.7 Full Abstraction and Semantic Equivalence
%% ============================================================
\section{Full Abstraction and Semantic Equivalence}\label{sec:full-abstraction}

\subsection{Semantic Equivalence}\label{sec:semantic-equivalence}

\begin{definition}[Semantic Equivalence]\label{def:semantic-equiv}
  Two programs $p_1$ and $p_2$ are \emph{semantically equivalent} (written
  $p_1 \equiv p_2$) if they have the same denotation:
  \[
    \sembrack{p_1} = \sembrack{p_2}
  \]
\end{definition}

\subsection{Observational Equivalence}\label{sec:observational-equiv}

\begin{definition}[Observational Equivalence]\label{def:observational-equiv}
  Two programs $p_1$ and $p_2$ are \emph{observationally equivalent}
  (written $p_1 \simeq p_2$) if no context can distinguish them:
  \[
    \forall C.\, C[p_1] \Downarrow v \iff C[p_2] \Downarrow v
  \]
  where $C$ ranges over all program contexts and $\Downarrow$ denotes
  convergence to a value.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Full Abstraction}\label{sec:full-abstraction-detail}

\begin{definition}[Full Abstraction]\label{def:full-abstraction}
  A semantics is \emph{fully abstract} if denotational equivalence coincides
  with observational equivalence:
  \[
    \sembrack{p_1} = \sembrack{p_2} \iff p_1 \simeq p_2
  \]
  \end{definition}

Full abstraction is the ``gold standard'' for semantic models: it means
the semantics distinguishes programs exactly when they can be distinguished
by observation.

\begin{theorem}[PCF is Not Fully Abstract in CPO Model]\label{thm:pcf-not-full}
  The standard CPO model of PCF (Programming in Computable Functions) is
  \emph{not} fully abstract.  There exist programs that are observationally
  equivalent but have different denotations.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
  The parallel-or function $\texttt{por} : \Bool_\bot \to \Bool_\bot \to
  \Bool_\bot$ is definable by:
  \[
    \texttt{por}(\mathbf{true}, y) = \mathbf{true} \quad
    \texttt{por}(x, \mathbf{true}) = \mathbf{true} \quad
    \texttt{por}(\mathbf{false}, \mathbf{false}) = \mathbf{false}
  \]
  This function is continuous but not definable in sequential PCF.  Two
  programs that differ only in their parallel-or behavior are
  observationally equivalent in sequential PCF but have different
  denotations in the CPO model.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Solutions to Full Abstraction}\label{sec:full-abstraction-solutions}

Several solutions have been proposed:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Game semantics}: Abramsky, Hyland, and others showed that
    game semantics provides a fully abstract model for PCF.
  \item \textbf{Logical relations}: Parametric logical relations can
    characterize observational equivalence.
  \item \textbf{Abstract machines}: Fully abstract abstract machines
    (e.g., the CEK machine for PCF).
\end{itemize}

\begin{insight}
Full abstraction is not just a technical property; it has deep philosophical
significance.  A fully abstract semantics captures exactly what can be
observed about programs---no more, no less.  This is the strongest possible
correspondence between mathematical meaning and observable behavior.
\end{insight}

