%% 25.8 Comparing Semantic Approaches
%% ============================================================
\section{Comparing Semantic Approaches}\label{sec:comparing-semantics}

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Comparison of Semantic Approaches}
  \label{tab:semantics-comparison}
  \begin{tabular}{llll}
    \hline
    \textbf{Property} & \textbf{Operational} & \textbf{Denotational} & \textbf{Axiomatic} \\
    \hline
    Meaning & How it executes & What it denotes & What it satisfies \\
    Mathematical basis & Transition systems & Domain theory & Logic \\
    Compositionality & Partial & Full & Full \\
    Handles nontermination & Yes (small-step) & Yes ($\bot$) & Indirectly \\
    Handles concurrency & Yes (small-step) & Yes (powerdomains) & Difficult \\
    Proofs about programs & Induction & Fixed-point induction & Hoare logic \\
    Implementation guide & Direct & Indirect & None \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{When to Use Each Approach}\label{sec:when-to-use}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Operational semantics}: When you need to reason about
    execution, prove type soundness, or define a language precisely.
  \item \textbf{Denotational semantics}: When you need a mathematical
    model, prove program equivalence, or build compiler correctness proofs.
  \item \textbf{Axiomatic semantics}: When you need to prove program
    correctness (pre/postconditions, invariants).
\end{itemize}

%% ============================================================
%% 25.9 Summary
%% ============================================================
\section{Summary}\label{sec:denotational-summary}

Denotational semantics provides the most mathematically rigorous approach
to programming language semantics.  By mapping programs to mathematical
objects (continuous functions on domains), it enables precise reasoning
about program meaning, equivalence, and transformation.

The key mathematical tools are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Domain theory}: CPOs and continuous functions provide the
    foundation for handling nontermination and recursion.
  \item \textbf{Fixed-point theory}: The least fixed point theorem ensures
    that recursive definitions have unique, well-defined meanings.
  \item \textbf{Powerdomains}: These handle nondeterminism by representing
    sets of possible outcomes.
  \item \textbf{Full abstraction}: This is the gold standard for semantic
    models, ensuring that denotational equivalence matches observational
    equivalence.
\end{itemize}

Denotational semantics has had a profound influence on programming language
research.  It has inspired the development of domain-specific languages,
compiler correctness proofs, and the theory of programming language
semantics more broadly.  While operational semantics is more commonly used
for defining languages, denotational semantics provides the deep
mathematical understanding that underpins our field.
